 =\\frac{0\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 5}{\\sqrt{34}} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\sqrt{34}}{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354478 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354478 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86537" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354478 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354478 #edb/ref 1071 "In what direction does the function $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ increase most rapidly at the point $(0,1)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354478 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d1b68e57-d9ea-56e4-b3b2-4bb6dcbf9139" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354478 #edb/ref 1074 "At a given point, a function $f(x,y)$ increases most rapidly in the direction of its gradient at that point.\n\nSo, we compute $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+6x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }i+(3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =(1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+6\\cdot 0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\text{ }i+(3(0)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function increases most rapidly in the direction of $-j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446175 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨2{e}^{4},\\,2{e}^{2}+3,\\,12⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446175 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446175 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01fea9f5-7ff4-5f0d-b619-5f87a6a90c35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446191 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨-3,\\,-4,\\,4⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446191 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446191 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4a640dc4-f0d6-5221-9834-4d0e7ba9f870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921864